/** @page hashdecl Hashdecl: Type-Safe Hash Declarations

    @tableofcontents

    @section hashdecl_overview Type-Safe Hashes Overview
    The <tt><b>hashdecl</b></tt> keyword allows for type-safe hashes to be declared; they can then
    be instantiated with the @ref new "new" operator, the @ref cast "cast<>" operator, or with
    @ref variable_implicit_construction "variable implicit construction".

    @section hashdecl_syntax hashdecl Declaration Syntax
    <tt><b>hashdecl</b></tt> <em>hashdecl_identifier</em><tt>[&lt;</tt><em>type_parameter</em><tt>[, ...]&gt;]</tt> <tt>[<b>inherits</b></tt> <em>parent_hashdecl</em><tt>]</tt> <tt>{</tt> \n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;<em>@ref data_type_declarations "member_type"</em><tt> </tt><em>member_name</em> <tt>[= </tt><em>@ref expressions "initialization_expression"</em><tt>];</tt> \n
    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;[...] \n
    <tt>}</tt>

    At least one member must be defined in the hashdecl or inherited from a parent; it's not possible to declare an empty type-safe hash.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
hashdecl MyHash {
    int i = 1;
    string code = "other";
}
    @endcode

    @note
    - a \c hashdecl may not have the name \c "auto", this name has a special meaning in complex types
    - Each %Qore type has a "pseudo-class" associated with it; for hashes the type is @ref Qore::zzz8hashzzz9 "<hash>"; methods from the data type's "pseudo-class" can be run on any value of that type.

    @section hashdecl_creation Type-Safe Hash Creation
    When type-safe hashes are created, the hash is automatically populated with the values given by
    the initialization expressions in the <tt><b>hashdecl</b></tt> declaration (if any).

    It is possible to override these declarations by passing a hash to be used for initialization;
    this can be passed the single optional argument to the type-safe hash initialization as in the
    following examples:

    @par Examples
    @code{.py}
# type-safe hash declaration
hashdecl Container {
    int i = 1;
}

# immediate value with implicit construction: default values are assigned from the declaration
auto ah1 = hash<Container>{};

# immediate value with implicit construction: default values are assigned from the declaration
auto ah2 = <Container>{};

# immediate value with implicit construction: overrides the "i" member's initial value
auto ah3 = <Container>{"i": 2};

# implicit construction: default values are assigned from the declaration
hash<Container> h1();

# implicit construction: overrides the "i" member's initial value
hash<Container> h2(("i": 2));

# "new" construction: default values are assigned from the declaration
hash<Container> h3 = new hash<Container>();

# "new" construction: overrides the "i" member's initial value
hash<Container> h4 = new hash<Container>(("i": 2));
    @endcode

    In such cases, the initialization expression for the members being overridden is never executed and the
    supplied value is used instead.

    Note that it's illegal to assign a value to an unassigned lvalue declared as a typed hash; in such cases a
    \c HASHDECL-IMPLICIT-CONSTRUCTION-ERROR exception is thrown as in the following example:
    @code{.py}
hash<Container> c;
# the following line will result in a HASHDECL-IMPLICIT-CONSTRUCTION-ERROR exception being thrown
c.i = 2;
    @endcode

    To address this, ensure that your typed hash lvalues are always initialized before assignment as in the following example:
    @code{.py}
hash<Container> c();
c.i = 2;
    @endcode

    @see
    - @ref variable_implicit_construction
    - @ref new

    @section hashdecl_generic Generic Hashdecls

    Hashdecls can declare formal type parameters and use them in member type
    positions. A generic hashdecl must be used with explicit type arguments in
    type positions such as <tt>hash&lt;Result&lt;int&gt;&gt;</tt>.

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
hashdecl Result<T> {
    string status;
    T value;
    list<T> values;
}

hash<Result<int>> int_result(("status": "ok", "value": 1, "values": (1, 2)));
int int_value = int_result.value;

hash<Result<string>> string_result(("status": "ok", "value": "done", "values": ("a",)));
string string_value = string_result.value;
    @endcode

    Type parameters can also be used in nested typed-hash members:

    @code{.py}
hashdecl Envelope<T> {
    string request_id;
    hash<Result<T>> result;
}

hash<Envelope<int>> response(("request_id": "abc", "result": int_result));
int payload = response.result.value;
    @endcode

    Generic hashdecl type parameters can declare defaults and upper bounds:

    @code{.py}
hashdecl Count<T: int = int> {
    T value;
}

hash<Count<>> default_count(("value": 1));      # same type as hash<Count<int>>
hash<Count<int>> explicit_count(("value": 2));
# hash<Count<string>> bad(("value": "x"));      # PARSE-TYPE-ERROR
    @endcode

    Generic hashdecl type arguments are invariant when concrete type arguments
    are used and are checked in the same way as other typed hash members. For
    example,
    <tt>hash&lt;Result&lt;int&gt;&gt;</tt> and
    <tt>hash&lt;Result&lt;string&gt;&gt;</tt> are different concrete hashdecl types.
    Omitting type arguments for a source-defined generic hashdecl, such as
    <tt>hash&lt;Result&gt;</tt>, is a parse-time type error.

    Wildcard type arguments can be used for read-oriented or write-oriented
    hashdecl annotations:

    @code{.py}
hash<Result<int>> result(("status": "ok", "value": 1, "values": (2,)));

hash<Result<?>> any_result = result;
hash<Result<? extends number>> numeric_result = result;
number value = numeric_result.value;
    @endcode

    Generic hashdecl inheritance can pass through type parameters or bind a
    parent to a concrete type argument:

    @code{.py}
hashdecl Parent<T> {
    T value;
}

hashdecl Child<T> inherits Parent<T> {
    string name;
}

hash<Child<int>> child(("value": 1, "name": "one"));
hash<Parent<?>> parent = child;
    @endcode

    Some builtin APIs also expose generic hashdecl result records. The type
    argument ties the record payload back to the producing generic class:

    @code{.py}
Queue<string> q();
q.push(value: "ready");
hash<QueueTryResult<string>> q_result = q.tryGet();
if (q_result.exists) {
    string value = q_result.value;
}

Channel<int> ch(1);
ch.send(value: 42);
hash<ChannelTryResult<int>> ch_result = ch.tryRecv();

hash<string, number> totals = {"net": 10.5};
HashPairIterator<number> it(totals);
while (it.next()) {
    hash<KeyValueInfo<number>> pair = it.getValue();
    printf("%s = %n\n", pair.key, pair.value);
}
    @endcode

    Reflection and API metadata preserve generic hashdecl type parameters,
    defaults, bounds, and parameterized parents. Tools should render
    <tt>hash&lt;Result&lt;int&gt;&gt;</tt> and
    <tt>hash&lt;KeyValueInfo&lt;number&gt;&gt;</tt> instead of the raw hashdecl
    name when the payload type is known.

    @section hashdecl_type_compatibility Type-Safe Hash Type Compatibility

    Type-safe hashes can be assigned to any variable that accepts an untyped @ref hash_type "hash",
    however variables declared as a particular type-safe hash (ex: <tt>hash&lt;MyHash&gt;</tt>) can
    only be assigned values of the given type; use the @ref cast "cast<>" operator to convert
    untyped hashes or type-safe hashes of another type to the target type for assignment.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
hashdecl Container1 {
    int i = 1;
    string str = "value";
}

hashdecl Container2 {
    int i = 2;
    bool check = False;
}

hash<Container1> c1();

# afterwards c2 will be: {i: 1, check: False}
hash<Container2> c2 = cast<hash<Container2>>(cast<hash>(c1 - "str"));

hash h = ("i": 3, "other": "x");
# afterwards c3 will be: {i: 3, check: False}
hash<Container2> c3 = cast<hash<Container2>>(cast<hash>(h - "other"));
    @endcode

    @see @ref cast "cast<>"

    @section hashdecl_inheritance Hashdecl Inheritance

    Type-safe hashes support single inheritance, allowing child hashdecls to inherit all members from a parent
    hashdecl. This is achieved using the \c inherits keyword.

    @par Example:
    @code{.py}
hashdecl BaseInfo {
    string name;
    int id;
}

hashdecl ExtendedInfo inherits BaseInfo {
    date created;
    *string description;
}

# Access inherited members
hash<ExtendedInfo> ext = <ExtendedInfo>{
    "name": "test",
    "id": 123,
    "created": now(),
};
printf("name: %s, id: %d\n", ext.name, ext.id);
    @endcode

    @subsection hashdecl_inheritance_rules Inheritance Rules
    - <b>Single inheritance only:</b> A hashdecl can inherit from at most one parent hashdecl
    - <b>All members public:</b> All inherited members are public (hashdecls have no access modifiers)
    - <b>No member shadowing:</b> Child hashdecls cannot declare a member with the same name as a parent member;
      this will result in a parse error
    - <b>Circular inheritance:</b> Circular inheritance (A inherits B, B inherits A) is detected and reported
      as a parse error
    - <b>Member initialization:</b> Parent members are initialized first, followed by child members

    @subsection hashdecl_inheritance_type_compat Inheritance Type Compatibility
    Values of a derived hashdecl type can be assigned to variables of the parent hashdecl type:

    @code{.py}
hashdecl Parent { int x; }
hashdecl Child inherits Parent { int y; }

hash<Child> c = <Child>{"x": 1, "y": 2};
hash<Parent> p = c;  # OK: Child can be assigned to Parent
printf("x: %d\n", p.x);  # Prints: x: 1
    @endcode

    @note The reverse is not true: assigning a parent hashdecl value to a child hashdecl variable requires an
    explicit cast.

    @section hashdecl_object_comparison Comparison of Type-Safe Hashes and Objects

    Type-safe hashes are similar to objects in that they have members, but there are some important
    differences as follows:
    - type-safe hashes are always passed by value whereas objects are always passed by reference
    - object classes can have methods, type-safe hashes cannot
    - object classes allow for class members to have access restrictions; type-safe hashes are made up exclusively of public members
    - object classes support multiple inheritance, type-safe hashes support single inheritance only
*/
